Implement strftime-style datetime format support in parse.py.

1. Add module-level helper functions in parse.py (near date_convert):
   - `_strftime_to_regex(format)`: converts a strftime format string to a regex pattern. Map each supported directive (%Y, %y, %m, %d, %H, %M, %S, %f, %z, %Z, %a, %A, %b, %B, %p, %I, %j, %U, %W, %c, %x, %X, %%) to a regex fragment. Escape literal text with re.escape. Raise ValueError for unsupported directives.
   - `_strftime_convert(string, match, format)`: parse `string` with `datetime.strptime(string, format)`. Determine return type: if format has both date and time directives -> datetime; only date -> date; only time -> time. Use a set of date directives (%Y%y%m%d%j%U%W%a%A%b%B%x%c) and time directives (%H%M%S%I%p%X%c).

2. Modify `Parser._handle_field` in parse.py:
   - After the `if not format:` block and before `extract_format`, add a check: if `format` starts with '%' and len(format) > 1, treat it as a strftime format.
   - Build regex via `_strftime_to_regex(format)`, set `s` to that regex, increment `self._group_index` by 1, set `self._type_conversions[group] = partial(_strftime_convert, format=format)`, and return `wrap % s`.
   - This preserves the existing `%` type (single '%' character) for percentage.

3. Add tests in tests/ (e.g., tests/test_strftime.py or extend existing test file):
   - Test `parse("a {:%Y-%m-%d} b", "a 1997-07-16 b")[0] == date(1997, 7, 16)`.
   - Test `parse("a {:%Y-%b-%d} b", "a 1997-Feb-16 b")[0] == date(1997, 2, 16)`.
   - Test time-only format returns time, datetime with timezone returns datetime with tzinfo.
   - Test mixing strftime fields with other field types.

4. Verify with `python -m pytest tests -o addopts=`.

Edge cases: invalid strftime directives raise ValueError; `%z` regex should match `+0000` or `+00:00`; `%Z` matches letters; `%c`/`%x`/`%X` use non-greedy `.+?` (may be imprecise but acceptable).

