Plan:
In `src/cachetools/_cachedmethod.py`, modify the `_DescriptorBase.__get__` method to handle the class-level access case where `obj is None`.

Add at the top of `__get__`:
```python
if obj is None:
    return self.Wrapper(None)
```
This avoids the subsequent `obj.__dict__.setdefault(...)` call that currently raises `AttributeError` (and for deprecated variants, the `_warn_instance_dict` DeprecationWarning) when `unittest.mock.create_autospec(SomeClass, instance=True)` introspects a `@cachedmethod`-decorated method on the class. Returning the wrapper is safe: all `Wrapper` subclasses only call `_WrapperBase.__init__`, which stores `obj=None` and only warns when `obj` is a type (not relevant), and the function signature is preserved via `functools.update_wrapper` in `_WrapperBase.__init__` (`__wrapped__`).

No other changes needed; real-instance behavior is completely unchanged because `obj` is never `None` for normal descriptor access.

Verification: run `python -m pytest tests -o addopts=` and ensure all existing cachedmethod tests pass with no new failures; optionally add a test in `tests/test_cachedmethod.py` that wraps `unittest.mock.create_autospec(SomeClass, instance=True)` in `warnings.catch_warnings` and asserts no warnings are emitted.

